From 387c43f51acebbdc745772e02e041bd50bc00a43 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iam <iam@iashur.internal>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 20:09:11 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] compositor: keep frame-timing history off the frame path
 (contention-free ring)

The honest FrameTiming from the previous commit was reverted on 782e72b for a
lock shape: GetFrameTiming is not a rare call -- a benchmark asks for it every
frame and an fpsVR-class overlay polls it continuously -- and a single mutex
around the whole history put that reader on the same lock as WaitGetPoses,
Submit and PostPresentHandoff. A caller merely asking what happened could stall
the frame that is happening.

The freeze that prompted the revert was NOT this: it was reproduced afterwards
on the stub build too, and reproduced again on Windows/SteamVR (the same
benchmark's second run in one process returns 354.77 FPS at 2576x2520 on a 3060
Ti, which is not a physical number). That belongs to the app's own second-run
path. But the lock shape was real, so it is fixed here rather than left standing
as a reason to keep the timing fictional.

One RwLock per ring slot instead of one around the history. The writer touches
only the slot it fills; a reader touches only the slot it was asked for. They
can meet only on the same slot, which needs the reader to be a full ring behind
the writer -- 128 frames, ~1.4 s at 90 Hz -- and both critical sections are a
copy of a handful of floats. An AtomicU64 of total frames completed doubles as
the length, so no separate count can fall out of sync, and it is published with
Release after the slot is written.

Deliberately not a seqlock: that is the textbook answer and would make the
writer strictly wait-free, but it needs UnsafeCell and hand-reasoned orderings
and nothing measured justifies that yet. Named as the upgrade if contention is
ever actually observed.

New test drives a writer thread and a reader thread against the ring with every
field of an entry derived from its index, so a torn read is detectable without
depending on timing. 88 tests pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
